we want to ask the question how should  we be teaching about climate change and  bruce i'm going to start with you on  this  should climate change be part of the  curriculum you're in the united states  at the moment what happens there at the  moment  so  um  this year has been the climate change  has been coming through the front door  like a battering ram right one in three  americans has been impacted by climate  change this year either super storms or  wildfires  and i have an eight-year-old daughter  she reads the headlines she understands  something is wrong and so it is critical  we help explain to our children science  of climate change why is it happening  and what do we need to do to solve it so  that they  as they become voters and actors in um  in the future that they are a part of  the solution and understand what we need  to do to help solve this problem so  education is absolutely critical i  didn't learn about climate change until  i went to university um that's too late  we need everyone including  our children in high school or grammar  school to be helping solve this problem  today  joyce what do you think about this  because i just want to show one  statistic to our viewers um one in five  children in the uk reports nightmares  about climate change i mean it's a  difficult subject to broach isn't it  with young children when  some of the news around it seems quite  scary  yeah and as bruce says children are  they're not you know they're not hidden  they're not um you know they they read  the news they understand what's going on  um and it's actually up to us the adults  the schools their parents to support  them on that journey and to be honest  with them about what is going on and why  it's happening and i think a huge part  of alleviating this anxiety is actually  taking action and being able to see  adults their leaders governments take  action um and actually  sit in their roles  of making positive change that is going  to be inspiring and that is going to be  empowering and it is going to take away  from this huge amount of fear that there  is right now because that fear is coming  um it's stemming a lot from this  helplessness and this overwhelm that  things aren't going to get better well  one way that children have actually  taken action that we've seen around the  world is these school strikes uh bruce i  mean do you approve of those there are  plenty of people here that worry that  children shouldn't be missing out on  vital education and missing days of  school and if they're gonna have their  voices heard why not do it at the  weekend  well so one of my most um  important memories of growing up was um  and similarly a a terrifying time during  um  the nuclear disarmament debate in the  1970s and 1980s one of the most  impactful things was going with my  church group down to london and hyde  park and and urging the government  uh to address the threat of nuclear  war  having children engaged to me was just a  critical step of understanding it and  also understanding that we can do  something about it so i'm a very big fan  of helping  inspiring children to get engaged in  civic engagement so that they can be a  part of shaping the future  and just another quick word from you on  that do you think children should be  allowed time off school to go on marches  or should they be spending time in the  classroom very briefly  yeah i don't think all education happens  in the classroom you have to get out  there you have to be part of it you have  to know what's going on in politics that  is also learning that is education for  them and so if that's what it takes to  get our leaders to make action and to  make children feel like they're part of  the solution then yeah i think it's uh  well worth the time okay well joyce  london and bruce nillis really good to  have you on the show thanks very much  indeed for joining us here tonight
 

